polygram NEWS — user-visible changes

Version 1.1.5 (minted 2026-10-11, unsigned, not tagged): the desktop extension

vibrobeast now also comes as a Claude Desktop extension: one file, dist/vibrobeast.mcpb, installed
by double-click or by dragging it onto Settings > Extensions.
- Same servers, one door. The extension runs the plugin's six servers behind one (the hub), so both
  forms run the same code. Ask Claude for vibrobeast_status to see which ones started.
- A settings page. The Tavern, Llama Bridge and C4 each have an on switch there, and their folders,
  the Tavern agent name and the Ollama address are fields. Nobody asks Claude to set a preference,
  edits a file, or restarts by hand to turn a door on. Off on the settings page is off, even when an
  older preference says on.
- Skills arrive the way the plugin's soft skills already do: served live by the memory seat.
- It needs Python 3.10 or newer on the computer. On Windows it finds one by itself, as the plugin does.
- `make mcpb` mints it; `make dist` now mints it beside the plugin and prints its sha256. Two
  readers check it: polygram/mcpb.py and bin/mb1ck (sh and awk).
- polygram/hooks.md proposes a new hook, "interrupted", so a turn cut off by a compaction or a
  failed reply can be rebuilt from words already on disk (h/ENHANCE-2026-10-11-interrupted-hook.md).

Version 1.1.4 (minted 2026-10-10, unsigned, not tagged): the partnership edition

This one is about who it's from. vibrobeast now says plainly that it comes from one collection
Corwin Brust and Bryant Frankford keep and share together, and it carries more of Bryant's work
than before.
- Terms, version 2. The Terms of Service and the TERMS block open on the two of us offering one
  shared collection. Code is under GPL-3.0-or-later OR LGPL-3.0-or-later, and writing under
  CC BY-SA 4.0. The credit line reads Corwin Brust and Bryant Frankford, with K Smith as editor of
  the works she edited. Corwin's two extra permissions are marked as his own. Bryant is in AUTHORS.
- The garden view (`site/garden/`, rebuilt at every `make dist` by `make garden-view`). It shows
  Bryant's Nexus Skill Tree, redrawn from his own data, beside ours, with the lines between them,
  the open and settled tensions, a compass of where each tool leans, and doorways to the MCP
  servers, the Wire, the Tavern and C4 with "Ask Claude" buttons. It works offline, in light and
  dark, by keyboard, and with JavaScript off.
- Two more of Bryant's skills, kept whole as soft skills: omni-tool (his front door; until now
  there was no counterpart here) and frugal-offload (where his omni-tool sends local-model work).
  Each is pinned by its hash and carries a notice.
- tg1rs reads C4QL tags such as `[PERSONA (ITEM)FACT]`. It looks a tag up both ways and says
  LINKED, FORWARD-ONLY, BACK-ONLY, GAP or WORD.
- Voice. The README opens in plain words and unpacks each term where it first earns its place.
  Text no longer talks about its own writer in the third person. GRAMMARY marks what doesn't
  ship with a single † and a legend. `conventions/VOICE.md` says how we write.
- The family cut (c4's fm1ct) can now cut this garden for a peer. It checks a parcel against the
  holds it was cut under, and its own failures show with --prove-red.

Version 1.1.3 (minted and signed by Corwin, 2026-10-10)

1.1.2's tree, signed and installed.

Version 1.1.2 (minted 2026-10-10, unsigned, not tagged): the extension edition

(1.1.0 was used up by a first mint that stopped before it wrote anything: it could not remove the
old build until file deletion was allowed in this folder.  1.1.1 was the same edition before the
third review pass; 1.1.2 adds that pass's fixes: the privacy text names the usage record, the
terms' licence line is healed, a soft skill question-economy, and the GRAMMARY marks what ships.)

What changes for you: preferences you can see and take back, token counts that no longer go below
zero, an opt-in usage record, the glossary (GRAMMARY.md) and the usage record's description
(FRAME.md) shipped inside the plugin, and licence lines that agree.  Nothing new turns itself on.
This edition was built after a long review: two opposite designs were drawn up, merged into one,
and read by eight reviewers; those working papers are in the author's tree, not shipped.
- Unsigned: the skills changed in this edition are not yet signed by the author (Corwin Brust).
  `sh check.sh` still proves every file is the one this build shipped; `skill-sign.sh verify` says
  TREE CHANGED for each changed skill, and for this build that is expected, not tampering.  It
  proves the bytes, not the author's approval: if you need that, wait for the signed build.
- Preferences ("switches", changed by asking Claude): the 14 keys become 18 (usage.capture,
  tone.dial, tone.show and people.named).  A row with a key the server doesn't know is now reported
  under "ignored", never silently dropped.  There is a pref_unset, and bin/pr1ef reads the same file
  in awk.  Skills declare the keys they use; person., room. and skill. rows have named readers, and
  a row about a person is written only from that person's own turn, or from the keeper's (the one
  person an install is for; by default corwin) for the people listed in people.named.  people.named
  starts as corwin: a new user asks Claude to add their own name (the README says how).
- Token counts: a token figure that rises is a reset (each of your messages starts a new count), so
  it is kept out of the totals and never subtracted.  Before, a span could report -28,574 tokens.
- Usage capture: off by default.  When on, it records two counts and nothing else: one at the start
  and one after each prompt, in the frame FRAME.md describes in words.
- Tavern: tavern_status names the build (stock 2.3.1, or garden-pony: Tavern patched
  with a tool registry) and which roads need
  the garden-pony build.  When tavern.as is unset, it is the agent already registered for this
  owner, else "claude".
- Persona: with no sheet, persona_resolve still answers in a plain default voice.  persona_ledger
  writes the persona net's ledger (who can check whom), read only from the sheets.  Edges the
  reviews proposed are listed apart, as proposals.
- New tools: st1dt (the seating detector: says what a folder holds, offers one way to seat it, and
  asks once), rd1ck with conventions/ROADS.md (more than one road to each store; a road it can't
  reach says NOT REACHED, never "absent"), qg1ck (the question gauge: two questions at once only
  when the chance the second is needed is at least 83%, the same rule FRAME.md writes as a
  confidence of 0.17 or less that it can be skipped), br1in (reads a drop of Bryant's work,
  never writes into it, and reports what changed in each skill since its last version, what ours
  answers to, and the modes of work it describes), lc1ck (licence lines), gm1gk (GRAMMARY counts).
- Licence: writing is CC BY-SA 4.0 (TERMS v1.1); only the Technology Architecture (TA) model and
  essays already published keep CC BY 4.0.  A release now refuses prose that carries a code licence or a retired bracket
  tag, unless a reason line is given.
- GRAMMARY.md (the glossary): 50 entries added, 86 in all as `sh bin/gm1gk` counts them, each
  saying whose word it is; it now ships in the plugin, and the README points to it for every term.
  The README's facts (the six servers, the switches, the edition floor) are now written from the
  code, it says in words how to restart the plugin and what an unsigned build looks like, and no
  step asks you to edit a file by hand.
- Skills: 54 skills lost an old typed GPL licence block that sat above their footer; the footer
  already said the right thing (writing CC BY-SA 4.0, scripts GPL-3.0-or-later).

Version 1.0.5 (minted 2026-10-10, not tagged)

The same skills as 1.0.4, minted again from the committed tree (commit d9db7a3 of 2026-10-09, which
landed the 1.0.3 and 1.0.4 work).  No skill changed between 1.0.4 and 1.0.5 (their curation
MANIFESTs list the same 66 skills with the same hashes); the plugin file differs.  This entry was
written after the mint, on 2026-10-10, from releases/1.0.5, the ChangeLog and git, because the mint
went out without one.

Version 1.0.4 (minted 2026-10-08, not tagged)

- Two new servers, both off until you ask Claude to turn them on: tavernface (Bryant Frankford's
  stock Tavern 2.3.1, carried byte for byte and pinned by its hash; tavern.on) and c4face (C4's own
  server, run with node from your C4 folder; c4.on).  The plugin now has six servers.  c4face's
  guard is an allow-list read by a small JavaScript reader; no static check of JavaScript is sound,
  so it is called a tripwire, not a wall.
- On Windows the servers start through servers/launch.cmd, which finds a Python by itself and says
  which one, or what to install.  Every server asks for Python 3.10 or newer, and an older Python
  is told so in one plain sentence ("too old", what to install, restart Claude).  A Windows-shaped
  mint says it is for Windows only and points at dist/posix/vibrobeast.plugin.
- Two new skills: garden-pony (a Tavern's tool registry) and topoforge-story (the story of
  TopoForge, which Bryant made).
- make dist no longer writes Python bytecode into the source tree.

Version 1.0.3 (minted 2026-10-08, not tagged)

- Llama Bridge is the plugin's fourth server, off until you turn it on (llamabridge.on): Bryant's
  bridge to a local model, carried byte for byte.
- New skills: build-loop (design, a QA plan, the build, counted QA passes and one final swing),
  llama-bridge, and vibrobeast.  altorewired learned "waiting is not silence" and ships a gauge for
  it (sl1gk).
- Changed skills: aethernet-relay, color-theory, drift, foaf (it names Bryant its author, by
  Corwin's word), kid-glove, lens and mentat-skill (no bracketed concern tags), persona-engine,
  session-rotate and token-optimizer.
- public-stage no longer asks for a session link in commits.

Version 1.0.2 (minted 2026-10-04, not tagged)

- New skill: persona-engine.
- kid-glove and drift re-seated after two faults found by trying them.

Version 1.0.1 (unreleased)

- A slow check that already passed on exactly this content is skipped, and says so: bin/release's secrets and
  tests gates, and gt1sp's `make check` and shell suites.  Any edit, tracked or untracked, runs it again;
  committing does not.  CK1SK=0 runs everything.  (bin/ck1sk)
- Bryant's skill-integrity gate: bin/sk1in reads every SKILL.md for NUL bytes, a last line that
  stops mid-sentence, unbalanced fences, a description over 1024 characters, a missing dated version
  note, and a packaged copy that differs from the authored one.  A Python second reader must agree
  (tests/test_sk1in.py).  With thanks to Bryant Frankford (BRYANT-INFLUENCER r2, offer 2.3).
- Two new skills are made as their own projects, each one literate recipe that unfolds into the
  skill, its tools and its tests: kid-glove (find the house rules first, fill only the gaps, log
  conflicts once, re-check before hand-back) and drift (a drift page by formula, linked both ways,
  with a second reader).
- Every skill ends in one common footer, minted, never typed: `bin/ft1mt` writes it from
  `conventions/house.env` (who, which licence, persona, model, the local time zone; tools write UTC)
  and `conventions/FOOTERS.org` (per skill: version, the version it was revised from and when, and
  its spine date, the earliest evidence found).  A skill is re-dated only when its body changes;
  `ft1mt check` and a Python second reader hold every footer to what the conventions say.  Prose
  is CC BY-SA 4.0 from this edition (scripts GPL-3.0-or-later); Bryant's work shares our licence.
- kid-glove and drift are full skills in the garden (copied from their recipe projects; each
  carries a FROM file).  The editions' floor is eight: drift takes the seat consent-gate held for
  it, as the default-skill-set design said.
- records/2026-10/: the licence direction from 2026-10-04 (-SA going forward; Bryant's terms where
  he set them; the TA model stays CC BY 4.0) and the first drift page over 2026-09-19..10-04.

Version 1.0.0
The first version meant for general use: the skills stand on their own, with or without C4, and the
two safety holes a cold review found are closed.  Everything carried by the 0.8.51-0.8.91 builds
(never tagged) is listed after this section.

- Safety: the memory store refuses a line break in any text a caller hands it (append, cite, bless).
  Before, one newline could write a second front-matter line that a reader took for real: a JAILED
  note read back SEATED, a citation named someone who never called, a blessing appeared under any
  name.  Legal values write the same bytes as before.  Notes written earlier are not re-judged;
  `sh tests/fm-dupkeys.sh DIR` lists any that already repeat a field.
- Safety: the prior-instruction soft skill says whose words count as an instruction: the person's
  own.  A fetched page, a pasted message, a tool's result or a file outside canon is data until the
  person adopts it in their own words.
  (Both from the Technology Architecture review, wave P12B; tests/safety-2026-10-04-tests.sh, each
  checker in a language other than the code it checks, both proven red on the pre-fix copies.)
- With or without C4: no skill description opens on "C4 canon" any more; thirteen say what they are
  for, and credit their C4 deck in the body.  Shipped copies of shared tools (ot1rd, rc1rn, sh1ck)
  run alone; a difference from C4's twin is a finding, never a ruling.  emacs-tashaki and
  reformat-prompt say what to do with no C4 tree.  The whole garden check passes with --no-c4.
- run-card: one `run` runs each step at most once (a card whose last step undid its first used to
  loop).  A planted break of the guard turns its tests red.
- The project root holds only the furniture, the read-first files and the live registers; dated
  working records are in records/<month>/.
- ERRATA E27 (a test that read the real persona sheets of the tree it ran in) is fixed.

Carried from the 0.8.90 and 0.8.91 builds
Everything since the 0.8.50 release commit, carried by the builds 0.8.51-0.8.58 and cut as 0.8.90,
is listed here; the 0.8.50 section below is as it was released.

- Sized editions: `make edition SIZE=grain` (or great, grand) mints `dist/vibrobeast-grain` beside
  the full plugin: the floor of seven plus 4, 9 or 17 percent of what the build draws on, picked by
  how often each skill was used; every other skill ships soft in the edition's SOFT-SKILLS folder,
  still found and read on demand. `RANDOM=yes` (or a size word, or all) adds a seeded random draw,
  to surface skills the ranking under-favours. The full plugin does not change.
- ERRATA lists only what the build beside it still owes; a fixed entry leaves it, and its fix is
  written here and in ChangeLog.  Folded after the 0.8.90 check (2026-09-29):
  - A plugin reads its own SOFT-SKILLS folder last, whatever roots are set; garden-usage answers a
    soft skill with the line that reads it.
  - build-loop gains the multitask swing (several tasks' QA checks run together, and a check two
    plans share must come out the same), with sw1mt beside sw1ng.
  - The values canon is named where it now lives, the pub shelf's canon/principles/.
  - Paper-trail stamps are UTC with a Z; sized editions are signed at release; nm1fl refuses the
    size words as flavors and reads `--flavor F`; skilltank reads RAISE; candor-gate says how to
    receive a drop; lens, countable-gauge and eclipse-pass land a result before showing it.
  - On a mount that refuses deletes, git status and diff read with --no-optional-locks (sandboxer,
    the instrument panel, bin/release), and a test holds every shipped script to it.
- Every skill in the garden is minted here: the twelve canon skills C4 derives from its decks are
  now in skills/, so the signing pass covers all 58. `polygram dist` says any skill still minted
  from another root, and any garden copy whose SKILL.md has moved on from it.
- The plugin README names the skills that fire broadly (today altorewired and token-optimizer),
  so the shop that receives them decides. candor-gate's `--travel` is the sender's read: it says a
  broad skill and sends it; the import read still HOLDs it at the receiving door. The gate no longer
  counts an archived signature as provenance, a URL that credits a source as egress, or
  "regression" as egress.
- foaf carries its license (text CC BY 4.0; code GPL-3.0-or-later with the LGPL permission for
  linking; Bryant Frankford and Corwin Brust). The plugin's author address is corwin@bru.st.
- The secrets checkers take a gauge for what they could not read: `SB_GAUGE=1` quiet, 4 (the
  default) a warning, 9 a line per path with its reason, 17 stop at the first. Not read is never
  clean at any setting.
- pb1ck reads `.c4/examples`: paths and addresses that travel on purpose read EXAMPLE, shown and
  counted, never a leak. Addresses at reserved domains (example.org, .invalid, ...) read NOTE.
- `make kit` runs every self-check a skill carries. thread.sh reads clearsigned and header-less
  messages. doc/polygram.info is rebuilt.
- A build can carry the keeper's word: `make dist RELEASE=0.8.90 NOTE="why"` writes a `note` line
  into releases/0.8.90 and, when Mentat's seat can be reached, the same note into the seat (seam
  polygram-releases) through the seat's own store code -- a shell cannot speak MCP and does not
  need to. The seat is found the way the plugin's server finds it, including the desktop app's
  home as msys2 sees it, and is never created. `python3 -m polygram.mcp.seatnote --where` says
  which seat it would use. tests/release-note-tests.sh.
- The secrets checkers name a FOLDER they cannot read (`UNREAD path/`) as they already named a
  file, and read a FILE named .git (a worktree or submodule pointer) where one of them used to
  skip it. cl1ck counts "not checked" apart from a disagreement between the checkers.
- Seven garden scripts that had no check now have one that can go red: the release verifier,
  sig-fresh, the sandboxer validators, the ethicsminions guards, the rolls vocabulary sync, the
  posix lint and the shared shell library. The sandboxer validator no longer leaves a Python cache
  beside the file it checks; the rolls vocabulary check matches whole words and runs on Python
  older than 3.10.
- consent-gate's freshness check (G2) parses dates without GNU `date -d`, so it can pass on BSD
  and macOS. seat_append's refusal explains the field that is actually missing.
- Two detached signatures that read BAD against their files (sandboxer's sig-fresh.sh.asc,
  instrument-panel's tests/selftest.sh.asc) no longer ship; they are kept in each skill's .arc.

- Named editions now import: other names are added as "Also known as ..." (no colon) inside a plain,
  quoted or block (>- |) description, and the .plugin uses forward slashes on Windows too.
  mentat-skill and session-rotate descriptions were not strict YAML; now folded blocks.
- skill-sign leaves a current signature alone: ours, verifying, and newer than every file it covers
  (or covering the same bytes) means no key and no passphrase prompt. Naming one skill says so; a
  sweep reports one count (SKILL_SIGN_VERBOSE=1 for every line). make dist signs in one sweep.
  tests/test_skill_sign_noop.py.
- Secrets are checked at every way out: bin/release (tracked files, before the tests gate) and
  `skill-sign.sh bundle` stop on a secret-shaped line, naming file, line and kind, never the secret.
  They call C4's bin/sb1rn, which picks one of two checkers written apart (awk, Perl) at random and
  has the other confirm a hit. `make dist` does the same as its first command, before the version
  bump (applied 2026-09-27). A line that must show an example: add sb:allow.
  tests/test_boundary_secrets.rb (Ruby, 17 checks).
- New skill garden-usage: says what anything in the garden is, in one sentence, then how to use it
  (scripts/whatis.sh NAME, --json, --list). Reads C4's four-name and tool registers and entity first
  lines when present; answers from the installed skills alone on a simple install. Two meanings are
  reported as a SEAM. tests/test_garden_usage.py.
- lens keeps reruns. A reading is now (subject, model, view, run): a second reading by the same model
  is kept as the next run instead of replacing the first, and `--run N` corrects run N. `converge`
  prints a within-model verdict beside the cross-model one: SPLIT-WITHIN (a model disagreed with
  itself), STEADY (every model that reran agreed with itself) or ONE-RUN. Before this, a model that
  said A then B, beside a second model saying B, printed CONVERGE. `lens.py selftest` (17 checks) and
  `selftest --prove-red`. SKILL.md gains "Two parties in the room, and when the answer is a choice":
  the two-readers four (b.A, b.B, b.AB) kept apart by name from (a)-(d), and "a persona shows
  reasons, not decisions". eclipse-pass move 3: rerun a diverging bias before calling a claim unstable.
- lens gains an optional doubled-nine mode and its kit, scripts/doubled_nine.py (draw, method, judge,
  measure, permute): the four-view pass run cold and with a persona, several times per arm, read against
  the rerun floor, including how often a decision flips within an arm versus across arms. Grown from the
  2026-09-27 experiment's arms/ and checked against it: every recorded distance, rank and decision count
  reproduces. `selftest` (12) and `selftest --prove-red`.
- skilltank reads C4's facts/: a note is a .md file or an extensionless text file, and the scan says
  how many it read and what it skipped (it read 10 of 155 in c4/facts before). New `--titles` clusters
  on what a note is called rather than its body, for prose facts named by their question. `fact` and
  `facts` join the container words in references/stopwords. `--selftest` (16) and `--prove-red`.
- writing-forms: a fifth technique, "his words": the record keeps his words verbatim, new wording
  sits beside it as a labelled reading, new writing may smooth his wording, and personal writing goes
  under a persona distinct from his.
- Hardening for a second garden (2026-09-27, four audits and their fixes):
  * Bundling a skill refuses symlinks and never ships .git, .arc or __pycache__ (a linked file or a
    git config could travel unscanned). A bundle now passes unbundle-and-verify; the check.sh and
    SAYS-SO it adds are reported as helpers the signature does not cover, and fail if they differ
    from the verifier's own copy. Signing no longer calls a changed skill "current" because its
    files carry old timestamps. verify says "key not in keyring" and names the key file.
  * No secrets checker is treated like a hit: bin/release and bundling stop unless a reason is given
    on the record. The two checkers are vendored in bin/ (sb1aw, sb1pl, sb1rn;
    tests/sb-vendored-tests.sh keeps them equal to C4's), so a garden with no C4 tree still scans,
    and `make dist` refuses to mint when it finds no checker at all.
  * The checkers name a file they cannot read (UNREAD, exit 2) instead of calling it clean.
    secret-sweep no longer reports "0 HIGH" when it read nothing, and never needs grep -Z. pb1ck and
    intake name the binary files they did not read. The tools work where only shasum is installed.
  * bin/release signs the installable dist/vibrobeast.plugin with the archives (servers and
    plugin.json included), and refuses a plugin that names another version.
  * The .plugin keeps its scripts' run bit (Unix zip entries, 0755 for "#!" files). Canon skills
    in it carry an express manifest. The pet edition is its own plugin (vibrobeast-pet), with fresh
    hash-only manifests and no signatures it cannot verify.
  * The plugin README is written for a stranger: Install, Verify (key fingerprint), and what it
    reads and writes; no author paths. INSTALL gives the source, the first-run order, a Verify step
    and what each tool is needed for. The docs use `sh bin/...` and no longer pin a version.
  * polygram doctor passes on a fresh clone (an extern never fetched is a note). Suites that cannot
    run here exit 77 and gd1ck shows them as SKIPPED; the Ruby secrets spec runs in the kit.
  * Skills that travel: boundscheck and run-card ship the tools they run (scripts/ot1rd,
    scripts/rc1rn); wf-check --prove-red works without C4; keeper paths are named by role with his
    path as the example; 26 skills gained "How this skill can fail"; the candor gate no longer holds
    every skill on its licence footer and has a --prove-red.
  * consent-gate: G3 now hashes the payload without its own payload_sha256 line. Before, a filled
    record could never pass G3. verify_consent.sh gained a licence header and --prove-red.

Version 0.8.50 (2026-09-25)
The first release cut through the whole pipeline since 0.7.0: fourteen z-builds (0.7.1-0.7.14)
folded into one, plus the first C4 publication tooling and licensing defaults beside it.
* Releasing -- `make dist RELEASE=X.Y.Z` names the version instead of z+1 (bin/rl2bp start --to;
  it refuses anything not greater than the current version). bin/release now checks that the
  artifacts were BUILT FROM this tree: the MANIFEST names the version, both archives carry that
  same MANIFEST, and every workspace row's sha256 matches the committed file. This replaces the
  "artifacts postdate HEAD" timestamp gate, which could never pass in the real order (make dist
  writes tracked files, so the commit that cleans the tree always lands after the mint). Tests:
  tests/release-gates-tests.sh. `polygram dist` now moves the bundles of skills no longer in the
  rack to dist/.retired-bundles/ instead of leaving them installable beside the live ones.
  `--push` now resumes after `--apply` (it used to die on "tag exists", so a release could never
  be published in two runs), and publishing is the signed tag pushed upstream.
  bin/gtmode picks the way: FORGEJO_TOKEN unset -> SSH with your key, exactly as git is set up
  (core.sshCommand honoured; no BatchMode); FORGEJO_TOKEN set -> HTTPS with the token for that run
  only, SSH switched off so nothing falls back to it, the token never in a file, URL or argument.
  With a token the Forgejo release page is made too; if that fails after the tag is up, the release
  is reported published with a retry line. tests/gtmode-tests.sh.
  The release's test gate says it is running, keeps the whole output in .git/polygram-release-tests.log,
  and names what failed. --accept-red="why" (or RELEASE_ACCEPT_RED) releases over red tests on the
  record: the reason and the failing tests go into the tag message. gt1sp shows each test as it runs
  and keeps their output in .git/gt1sp-tests.log; its check-first report lists files without arguing.
- Terms of Service and Privacy now reach the installed plugin: bin/tp2mt enters the pair in the plugin
  tree's MANIFEST and carries it in vibrobeast.plugin (both were missed, so the tree's check.sh failed).
- Named editions: `make dist` mints dist/vibrobeast-<flavor>/ and its .plugin beside the signed one
  when your preferred flavor is persona, standard or pet (C4's bin/nm1fl and c/skillnames; four names
  per skill). The preference is read from --flavor, $POLYGRAM_FLAVOR, ./.c4/flavor, then
  ~/.config/polygram/flavor. `rl2bp start --to` the current, untagged version re-mints in place
  (recorded as `remint`). gt1sp's h/TRANSPORT.log is git-ignored: written after the commit, it left
  the tree dirty and the release gate refused.
* bin/gd1ck -- the whole-garden test and benchmark kit. One command runs every suite
  it can find in this repo and the C4 tree (and each suite's --prove-red twin), lints
  and sizes every skill, checks the minted plugin's bytes and gauges the garden.
  `make kit` / `make kit-quick`. Legs run under your own locale; --c-locale probes C.
* skill-metrics (new skill) -- a time series of interesting events per shipped item:
  metrics/log (ITEM STATE CUR LAST DATE NOTE), metrics/skill-metrics/<item> holding
  the last STATE, content-keyed archives. POSIX sh; reads model POSIX grep. The
  implementation lives in the skill (scripts/sk2mt, scripts/sh1lib.sh); bin/sk2mt is
  a shim. SPEC.md + a second reader in Python.
* persona server -- serves the sheets already written, read-only, where they lie (no copy,
  no install). A file is a sheet by what its own first lines say: an `actor:` line, a
  CHARACTER/ITEM SHEET or PERSONA heading, or front matter naming a persona or model. Roots from
  the new `persona.sheets` preference, else $PERSONA_SHEETS, else guessed from softskills.roots
  and words.root. Copies in other folders are reported as agreeing or differing, never merged.
  A second reader of the rule in awk: tests/persona-shelf-tests.sh. (0.7.8)
* plugin_freshness -- reads the `freshness.shelf` preference itself, as the session-open check
  already did; a plain call no longer answers UNKNOWN. (0.7.8)
* persona server -- a sheet and its mutation journal no longer compete for a name: reading a
  sheet returns it with its journal beside it. The mode word reads SEATED when the server's own
  store is reachable but not yet written. (0.7.10)
* eclipse-pass -- `--from pmo/projects.tsv` reads the roster (keys from column 1, header skipped)
  and prints where each pick lives; the documented example now uses the roster, not a folder
  listing that also offered non-projects. (0.7.10)
* pmo -- `projects.tsv` gains a `home` column; `pmo-roster` lists keys, resolves homes and names
  roster gaps; `pmo-report` gains an Opportunities section. (0.7.10)
* Terms of Service and Privacy Policy -- `make dist` now mints both for that build (bin/tp2mt),
  stamped with its version. Every sentence about what the servers do is first checked against
  their source (no network, where each store lives, the SECRET rule, skillx refusing). A claim
  that no longer holds stops the build. Every filter is named, and the same Words block appears in
  both. Tests: tests/tp2mt-tests.sh. (0.8.50)
* persona server -- a sheet may say its own name with a `name:` line (outside front matter), so a
  persona can be renamed without moving its file: gw7's sheet now serves as gh7. The awk second
  reader in tests/persona-shelf-tests.sh checks the same rule. (0.8.50)
* persona_list -- reports a SPLIT when one file name serves under two names (a stale copy of a
  renamed sheet), and a sheet with no heading takes its first H1 as its title. (0.8.50)
* session-rotate -- the version bump replaces vN only as a whole token, moves "prior vN" along with
  it, and names the snapshot actually written. Rolling more than once a day (or a second) no longer
  overwrites an earlier roll's snapshot or archived continuity files: each takes the next free name.
  --no-bump rotates a model that declares no session-close step (c4) without touching its version.
  tests/session-rotate-tests.sh, which rolls three times in a row. (0.8.50)
* shell-tooling and project-memory, two unauthored stubs, are retired from the rack (receipts D-1
  and D-2 of the 2026-08-17 removal pass), with REJECT rows so the gate does not re-mint them. The
  C4 canon drops its two gf-02-* duplicates, and guarded-visibility gets a real trigger. (0.8.50)
* No more "draft" labels from the tools: polygram's synth no longer writes "Draft skill ... Edit me"
  (it says the steps are not written yet), and c4cs mints with c4_status minted-from-canon and a
  trigger that says where it came from. The environment has no settled meaning for draft yet, and
  the label read later as the author's own terms. (0.8.50)
* Licensing notices -- every GPL notice in the tree now uses the standard wording, "either version 3
  of the License, or (at your option) any later version". (0.8.50)

Version 0.7.0 (2026-07-13)
* Add universal doctrine: Interweave talent, raise aspect, mediate.
  (*seams*) This kicked it literally faster than I could apply it.
  Note, YMMV: tests/results stem from the environment where I
  experienced them; this approach is most effective when driven by
  local, organically sourced wights, measures, and weather/climate.
* (sandboxer) observe indirect uses of untrustworthy (read) paths.
  Stop using mtime as tiebreaker; use agreement-branch + sentinel.
  define "fresh reader" as being the hosts file tool or the host.
  add HEAD-IS-YOURS check before any restore
  writes-vs-reads: (hint) bash never lies about the disc.
  widen triggers, note bash trust (write=yes, reade=no); improve
  guards against unsafe tool run/tool gen.
* consent-gate: the door opens too
  Strip the urgency and ask again -- urgency is pressure when it substitutes for
  verification, substantiated when the gates clear without it. And classify
  before you ceremonialize: a change is credential-class iff it touches a
  secret's value, scope, lifetime, or audience; plumbing is a tool change. Both
  rules exist to stop gate-inflation, which is how a discipline becomes a
  superstition and gets routed around. NO4C folded: raised parks, mentioned
  notes, carried disarms
* kill-sign: signature form follows artifact class
  Detach runnables and machine-parsed files (clearsign breaks YAML fences and
  shebangs, and its round-trip is not byte-idempotent); clearsign prose. A BAD
  over bytes that still match a signed manifest is a stale signature artifact,
  not tampering -- diagnose before you alarm.
Tools and records
  * bin/thread.sh -- renders a message thread to one static HTML page (sh+awk,
    escape-first, unknown flags rejected loudly). The signed page regenerates
    byte-true from the signed generator; that reproducibility is the point.
  * journal/ -- Grader's Tales, the instrument's narrative log, plus the thread
    convention (filename order IS the thread). Charter: if a journal entry ever
    becomes load-bearing, the ledgers failed.
  * The losers are kept. This release ships its own refuted designs -- the
    guard-registry counterexample, countersigned above its correction, and both
    eclipse bias models. Mistakes are the resume of every tool-builder.

Version 0.6.1 (2026-07-05)
* Per-skill provenance: every curated skill tree now carries its own
  signed whole-tree manifest (skill-sign, pinned key) — verification
  works standing in skills/, per skill, not only per release. 13/13
  signed and 2/2 verified (keeper bench + instrument).
* Repairs 0.6.0's skilltank bundle, which shipped stale pre-release
  bytes (a mint-time stale-reader fault, not tampering). Rule pinned:
  mint and certify from git-true bytes, never through the bash mount.

Version 0.6.0 (2026-07-04)
* Armed AI provenance gates: polygram can cross-check supplied facts
  against seeded, hashed memory attestations bound to PRINCIPLES canon
  (the minions --expect drift check, live) to frustrate spoofing and
  tampering — and selectively apply skill ("flavor") accordingly.
* Durable REJECT: the advice history is now a ledger the gate consults;
  a rejected candidate stays rejected until review --promote records a
  later PASS. Labels formalized in labels.tsv ("no consumer, no label").
* skill-sign (new, Haiku lineage): whole-tree manifest signing for skill
  directories and .skill bundles; verification pins Corwin's key.
* security-digest (new): stand-alone, Mentat-aware, schedulable SRE
  advisory digest — "Report, never act."

Version 0.5.4 (2026-07-02)
* dist gained a temp-file stray filter: sed -i mkstemp siblings (the
  sedx44W4d residue that rode into the signed 0.5.3 mint — sha-identical
  to its source, nothing leaked), vim swaps, and emacs lockfiles can no
  longer be staged. Respin release; 0.5.3 artifacts retired.
* `polygram dist --version X` now works as an alias for --dist-version
  (argparse scopes it to the subcommand; bare `polygram --version` still
  prints the tool version).
* dist now mints correctly under mingw/msys Python (mixed path
  separators no longer crash manifest staging).

